    "q-164515": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164515",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Identify the surface $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis and $b=\\sqrt{5},\\,a=c=\\sqrt{2}.$",
